new file mode 100644
index 0000000..11da15b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/nextendo.md
@@ -0,0 +1,36 @@
+# Online play through Nextendo Network
+
+[Nextendo Network](https://nextendo.network) is a community server for Switch games. With `DM_NEXTENDO=1` (the launcher's **Nextendo online** row) the host takes a supported
+game online through it. Nintendo's servers are never contacted. Supported so far: **Super Smash Bros. Ultimate 13.0.5**. Every other game, and this one with the switch off,
+runs offline exactly as before.
+
+> **Status: written, not yet run.** The code follows how Super Mario Bros. 35 Recomp signs in to Nextendo and how WindowSmash points Smash's NEX client at a server of its own.
+> It has not been built with Xcode or played. Expect fixes on the first runs; `DM_NEXTENDO_LOG=1` shows each step.
+
+## Before the first run
+
+1. **A Nextendo account** at nextendo.network.
+2. **Your own application** at nextendo.network → Developers → New application: public (PKCE), redirect `http://127.0.0.1:47823/callback`, scopes `identity` and
+   `game.matchmaking`. Put its client ID in `DM_NEXTENDO_CLIENT_ID` (or `NX_CLIENT_ID_DEFAULT` in `dm/host/net_online.h`). Do not reuse another project's client ID.
+3. **Nextendo's addresses for Smash.** The defaults (`NX_SERVER_DEFAULT`, `NX_NAT_DEFAULT`, `NX_PIN_DEFAULT`) are the ones Super Mario Bros. 35 Recomp ships. Ask Nextendo
+   whether Smash uses the same game server, NAT check and certificate key; if not, set `DM_NEXTENDO_SERVER`, `DM_NEXTENDO_NAT` and `DM_NEXTENDO_PIN`.
+4. **Rebuild the game** so `main` gets the login gate (below). The build prints a red line when the gate was left out.
+
+## How it works
+
+| Piece | Where | What it does |
+|---|---|---|
+| Switches, names | `dm/host/net_nextendo_apple.m` | `DM_NEXTENDO*`; every `*.nintendo.net` name resolves to Nextendo's game server (`nncs2-*` to their NAT check), nothing else resolves |
+| Account | `net_nextendo_apple.m` | OAuth 2.0 authorization code + PKCE in an in-app sheet (iPhone) or the browser (Mac), redirect caught on `127.0.0.1`; refresh token in the Keychain; `GET /api/nex-token` gives the NEX login token and the PID |
+| ID token | `net_nextendo_apple.m`, `hle_account.c` | the network service account's ID token: a JWT shaped like Nintendo BaaS's with the PID as `sub` and the NEX token as `nnex` |
+| Sockets | `dm/host/hle_net.c`, `hle_offline.c` | `nn::socket` on the host's BSD sockets (non-blocking at the host; the game's blocking calls wait in slices). TCP reaches Nextendo only, UDP any address (Pia's peers) |
+| nifm | `hle_net.c`, `hle_offline.c` | the network is available; the IP configuration is the host's |
+| NEX login | `dm/games/01006A800016E000/patches.py`, `hle_net.c` | gate `OBS_NEX_LOGIN` on the login job gives NEX's NgsFacade a direct server address, `10.13.37.1:443`, where NEX speaks its WebSocket without TLS |
+| TLS bridge | `net_nextendo_apple.m` | a TCP connection to `10.13.37.1` is a local socket whose other end goes to the game server in TLS (Network.framework), the server's key pinned, the `Host` header rewritten |
+
+## Known gaps
+
+- Friends, presence, play reports, BCAT and `nn::ssl` stay cut. If Smash needs one of them before it reaches the Online menu, that hook is the next piece of work.
+- The login gate's offset and the facade layout are for `main` 21450c64… (13.0.5) only.
+- Pia's NAT traversal runs on the host's UDP sockets unchanged; a network that blocks peer-to-peer UDP (some mobile networks) will not reach matches.
+- Tokens, codes and the PID are never logged. The players in a match learn each other's IP address, as on a console.
